WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:02.950
- Are you interested in
a career in consulting?

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:06.210
Most people that I know that
interview for consulting roles

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.780
have one thing that
they are most scared of,

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.860
and this is the case interview.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.640
So welcome to another video here

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.590
on my channel Firm Learning,

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:17.530
my name is Heinrich, I'm a
former McKinsey consultant

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:20.660
and today let me share a
couple of case interview tips.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.330
And specifically we're going to look

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:25.880
onto the three main components
of most case interviews.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.070
First, the qualitative part.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:31.530
Second, the data reasoning part and third,

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.270
the quantitative part.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.250
So surely formats differ from before

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:37.990
but I'd argue that these things are part

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:40.290
of most of these case interview formats

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.770
so let's get into it.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.370
The purpose of the case interview

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.210
in a consulting interview process

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.170
is to test how comfortable
you are with situations

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:50.710
that you will face as a consultant.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:52.100
So the interviewer will present you

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.020
with it a business problem

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.960
and then expect you to solve it.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:58.030
Sometimes in the more unstructured way,

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:01.300
sometimes we're going over
a specific set of questions

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.240
that the interviewer has prepared for you.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:06.460
And one key part that is often
the case in these interviews

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.050
is what I like to call data reasoning

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:11.210
and this means that at
some part in the interview,

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.410
the interviewer will show you a chart,

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.020
will give you a handout.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.180
If it's a video interview
often will just share

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.780
his or her screen to show you some data,

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:21.450
some data visualization,

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:22.540
and of course this is what

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:23.690
you will be faced with.

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:26.610
Quite frequently you will
work with lots of data

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:29.840
in consulting, and the key
question is, how good are you?

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.920
How are your effective value
at interpreting this data?

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.570
And getting insights out of it.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.100
So let's look into an example.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.450
Let's imagine the interviewer
would show you this exhibit

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.090
and the case context is,

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.170
that you are asked to
look into opportunities

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.060
for this company to grow its revenue

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.420
and its profit over the next years.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.220
And now you're looking at this chart

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.340
and the interviewer might ask you,

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.920
well, what do you see here?

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.090
What do you make of this?

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:54.140
Any applications you can draw from this?

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.890
And in my humble opinion
in situations like those,

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:59.700
there are three quality levels of answers

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.320
that candidates give you.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.320
Let's start with the lowest quality level,

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:04.880
the lowest level of insight.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.630
And this is just restating the facts.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:10.040
So surely if you look
at a slide like this,

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:11.100
you see lots of facts.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.030
You see for instance in year one,

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.690
that product A had net
sales of 100 Euro million

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:18.490
and that 20 million units of product A

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:19.660
were sold in year one

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.050
and that the company made a profit

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:22.610
of 20 million in that year.

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:25.190
And some candidates
will tell you just that.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:27.800
Some candidates will just read out things

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.330
that you can clearly read
on your slide anyway,

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.780
just tell you what exactly the numbers are

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:33.940
that they see there.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:35.480
And of course I hope you understand that

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:36.910
this is not the best answer.

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:38.710
This is really stating the obvious,

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:41.460
stating the things that everybody can say

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:42.750
by just reading the slide.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.840
So this is the first level of insight.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:47.820
Let's get to the second level of insight.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.410
And this is providing a qualified summary.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:51.360
Let's call it this way.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.480
So people look at the information

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:56.090
and do give you some good insights.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:57.613
Maybe they're able to conclude that

00:02:57.613 --> 00:03:01.620
well product A stayed constant
from year one to year two,

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.800
so no changes,

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.330
or maybe they were able to conclude

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.990
that product B doubled in sales.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:11.990
So from 150 million in year
one, to 300 million in year two,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.840
or that maybe product
C actually is declining

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.340
by apparently more than 15% here

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.500
in writing sales year on year.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.140
So this would be a second information.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:22.860
It's a restatement of facts,

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.970
a condensation of the
information that you get

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:29.380
and then restating this as
a little qualified summary

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:30.570
for the interviewer

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:33.320
and surely this is already
much better, right?

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.300
Than just reading all the facts

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.350
but I would argue there's one thing

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.870
that really the most
stellar candidates do,

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.310
the most outstanding candidates do

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:45.050
that is beyond just providing a summary.

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:48.000
And this is really
looking into the insights,

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.540
into the implications
of what you see here.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:53.030
Well it might be the case that
this is how the products are

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:54.670
that this is how they developing,

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:56.760
but what does this really mean

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.200
for the case question at hand?

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.460
What do you really make
out of that, right?

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:03.520
This I think is the main question.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.080
So always think back to
the original case question,

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.710
what did the interviewer
ask you to actually look at?

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:11.020
Well the question was,

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:13.300
how can this company
increase revenue and profit

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:14.460
over the next years?

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:16.330
So with this information in mind,

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:18.130
what do you make out of this?

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.020
And I think here it's one crucial thing

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:21.030
that you need to understand

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:23.680
that product C seems to be
the most important product

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.270
for the company in regards to the revenue,

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:28.980
but also in regards to the profit,

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.410
but product C is declining.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:34.110
Revenues are down, profit are down, right?

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:36.900
And while it is true that
product B is growing,

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:40.160
this does not even over
compensated the loss,

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:41.780
that product C is making.

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.540
So this is really a problem
that this company is having.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:46.010
You really need to investigate

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.110
what is going on with product C,

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:50.350
why isn't it performing that well anymore,

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:51.343
because if we don't fix this,

00:04:51.343 --> 00:04:54.350
it will be very difficult
for this company.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:56.510
And this is exactly what
the stellar candidate

00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:59.160
would have been able to
draw out, to see this,

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:01.980
to find this implication,
to find this award

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:04.100
and then link it back
to the case question.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.880
Don't just restate some facts.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.610
Don't just summarize things,

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:10.810
but make it clear what the
implications of that are

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:14.600
for the manager, for the CEO,
for your client tomorrow.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.080
Show that you are able to see this.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.710
Next is the quantitative
part of the interview.

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:19.850
So surely in consulting,

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:22.200
we work with data, with numbers a lot,

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.840
and it's always great that the interviewer

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:24.673
will look for that.

00:05:24.673 --> 00:05:26.540
Whether you are comfortable with numbers,

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:28.280
whether you are good at calculations.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:30.230
And here my first tip would be,

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:33.520
before you even get a math
question for the interviewer,

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:34.930
try to show whenever it makes sense

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:37.030
that you're really
comfortable with the numbers.

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:38.870
So here staying with this example,

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:39.840
it would have been great

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:41.580
that without even getting a question,

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.330
that you would have just
calculated for instance,

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:47.980
the unit price that this
company sells the products to.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.300
So for instance product A,

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.060
you can always divide
net sales by units sold,

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:55.520
then hundred over 20 would
mean a unit price of five Euro

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.970
or for product B 150 over 60

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:00.710
would be a unit price of 2.5 Euro

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:03.260
or product C 480 over 240

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.690
would be a unit price of two Euro, right?

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:08.370
So five, two and a half and two.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.310
And by doing that you could
have then also compared

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:11.883
whether the pricing would have changed

00:06:11.883 --> 00:06:13.920
between year one and year two,

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.390
because this of course
might've also influenced

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:18.820
how the different numbers
changed year on year

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:19.687
and if you did this,

00:06:19.687 --> 00:06:21.980
you would have realized that
the pricing did not change

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:24.490
the price stayed constant over time.

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:26.860
The similar thing that you
could just have calculated,

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.020
which would just have been
strong if you just mentioned this

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:31.090
without even anybody asking you for that,

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:34.340
it's just the profit margins
for these different products.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:35.780
So here for product A,

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:38.410
20 million profit over
a hundred million sales

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:40.700
would be a profit margin of 20%.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:42.710
Product B with 30 over 150

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:45.410
would also have been a
profit margin of 20%,

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:48.030
but then product C 240 over 480,

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:51.606
here we can see that this is
a profit margin of 50%, right?

00:06:51.606 --> 00:06:53.470
So 20, 20 50,

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:56.500
and this would have just stressed
and underlined your point

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:59.690
of saying that this company
really has a significant issue

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:02.330
because product C really
is the most profitable one

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:05.570
not only in absolute numbers,
but also in relative numbers,

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:08.880
providing a profit margin
of 50% for every unit

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.910
of this product C that
the company is selling.

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:13.650
Doing calculations like that
with mental math is of course,

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:15.580
very confident, very strong,

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:17.250
shows that you know what you're doing

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:18.980
and really have all the numbers in order.

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:20.190
What interviewers like to do

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:22.030
is then ask you to do some calculations

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:24.960
often also with numbers
provided on a chart exhibit

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:25.793
like this one.

00:07:25.793 --> 00:07:27.670
Here you need to know that
usually you're only allowed

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:29.150
to work with pen and paper,

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:32.287
which means that you should
practice written multiplication

00:07:32.287 --> 00:07:34.800
written division before you
walk into the interviewer

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:36.570
because even if you are good at math,

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:38.540
of more complex calculations

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:40.230
if you're not practice with doing them

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.380
on pen and paper without a calculator,

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:44.640
often you will really
mess up the calculations

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:46.430
and this is surely what you should avoid.

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:49.230
And here also be ready
to do these calculations

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:52.650
up to the first or second
or third decimal point.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:55.270
Sometimes interviewer just
want to see that you are able

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:57.910
to really get these calculations right

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:00.110
also to the first or second decimal.

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:02.010
Make sure you can do that

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:03.870
because this might be expected from you.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.770
The third area I wanna talk
about is the qualitative part

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:07.900
of the case interview.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:11.850
Here it will be about structuring
a problem into its issues,

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:14.570
into its drivers, all the
aspects that are relevant.

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:17.030
So in the context of this little
example you have for today,

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:18.150
the interviewer might ask you

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:21.790
to come up with all the relevant
drivers or decision factors

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:23.690
that influence the ability of the company

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:25.990
to really increase its revenue and profit

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:26.940
over the next years.

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:30.540
So this is a typical business-related
structuring question.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:32.240
And here now the key thing is of course

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:33.840
to have a structure that you use

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.800
to not just engage in
a brainstorming mood,

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:38.690
but to show that you are
able in a repeatable way

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:40.100
to create insights like that.

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.750
And one big error that many people do

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:43.500
is that they get all these books

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:45.650
with all these case prep frameworks,

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:50.350
and they learn 30, 40, 50,
60 frameworks or so by heart,

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:52.720
believing that just the
more frameworks they know

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:53.553
the better,

00:08:53.553 --> 00:08:55.630
and they just need to
learn the perfect framework

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:58.070
for every individual case
that exists out there

00:08:58.070 --> 00:08:59.090
just to be fine.

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:00.830
But this of course is quite dangerous

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:03.450
because it's very likely that
the case that you will get

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:07.360
just will not fit any specific
framework that you memorized.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:08.860
So I would not recommend you to learn

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:10.360
all these frameworks like that,

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:12.180
but I do think it is worthwhile to learn

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:13.670
a handful of frameworks,

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:15.930
which you would be able
to use quite often.

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:18.100
And I think here for
these qualitative purposes

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:19.750
probably the most powerful one

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.310
is a business situation type of framework.

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:25.180
So framework that you can use to structure

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:27.670
and analyze different factors

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:29.920
that affect business decisions.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:31.380
And they wondering the known one

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:35.240
is the so-called three C
framework or the three C model.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:38.010
So company, customer, competition.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:41.060
So for instance if you would
now analyze this specific case,

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:42.620
you would first want to learn more

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:44.270
about the company, your client.

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:46.500
So what are the capabilities
of the employees?

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:47.970
How was the organization look like

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:49.770
and what countries are they active?

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:51.800
How does their product
portfolio look like?

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:54.170
What's the pricing looking
like and so on and so forth,

00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:55.770
some will in turn affect us.

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:57.860
Second, customers, right?

00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:00.060
So what different customer segments exist?

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:01.430
What are their preferences?

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:02.780
Which segments are growing?

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:04.810
Which are declining?
And so on and so forth.

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:06.470
And then third competition.

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:08.160
So what is your market shareholders?

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:09.920
This maybe differ by region.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:12.380
How does your product
compare to their products?

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:15.220
What are the differentiating
factors USPs that you have

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:16.420
compared to them?

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:19.280
So just a couple of factors
that you can always go over

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:20.480
in these situations.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:23.630
An adaptation from there that
I always liked really to use

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:26.120
is to use this but then discuss product

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:28.670
in a separate bucket, in a separate area

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:30.800
because the problem that I
feel like a bit of you use

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:32.890
company, customer, competition is this,

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:35.050
that this is just so widely used,

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:37.680
so widely known, that
often the interviewer

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:39.490
will just get a bit bored, right?

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:40.323
If you start with this

00:10:40.323 --> 00:10:42.913
just because probably every
second or every third candidate

00:10:42.913 --> 00:10:44.590
will have a framework like that.

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:45.730
And for this specific reason,

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:47.440
I do think it also makes sense

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:49.640
that maybe you wanna think
about your own version

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:51.500
of organizing these factors,

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:53.800
just to make it a little
bit more innovative,

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:56.860
just to not make it seem
like the same structure

00:10:56.860 --> 00:10:59.230
that you just learned by heart
that everyone else is using.

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:00.550
Last but not least on this point

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:02.770
what's also important
is how you present it.

00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:06.500
And here my key advice is to
present in a top-down way,

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:09.550
top-down communication
is what it's all about.

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:11.750
If you have no clue at
all what this means,

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:13.480
I will link another video above here

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:15.330
where I explain this concept in detail,

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:18.350
but specifically for this idea,
here is what you need to do.

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:20.800
Always first start by
presenting the first level,

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:22.460
then the second level,
then the third level

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:23.293
and so on, right?

00:11:23.293 --> 00:11:24.760
So when you now present your structure,

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:28.320
start by saying, okay, I
looked into three things.

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:33.110
First, the company, second
customer, third competition.

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:34.240
You explain the structure.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:36.010
Then you go into the first bucket.

00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:38.010
So let's start with the company.

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:40.370
In the company, I have these elements

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:41.370
I'd like to talk about.

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:44.580
Element one, element two,
element three, element four.

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:46.260
So let's go into element one.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:47.370
This is what this is about.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.060
Element two, this is what this is about.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:51.630
So always keep the
pyramid principle in mind

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:53.440
there's other concept I'll also talk about

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:54.440
in this other video,

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:56.200
